## Chapter 6: Control Flow

Control flow in Zig looks familiar at first glance – `if`, `while`, `for`, `switch` – but each comes with subtle superpowers and intentional limitations. The language’s “no hidden control flow” principle applies here too: what you see is what executes. No operator overloading that secretly calls functions, no implicit truthiness conversions, no fall‑through in `switch` statements. This chapter dissects each construct, shows you how the compiler thinks about them, and reveals why Zig’s approach eliminates entire classes of bugs found in C, C++, and even Rust.

---

### 1. Basic Usage

#### `if` and `else`

The simplest conditional works exactly as expected:

```zig
const std = @import("std");

pub fn main() void {
    const x: i32 = 42;
    if (x > 0) {
        std.debug.print("positive\n", .{});
    } else if (x == 0) {
        std.debug.print("zero\n", .{});
    } else {
        std.debug.print("negative\n", .{});
    }
}
```

**Key difference from C:** The condition *must* be a `bool`. No `if (ptr)`, no `if (err)` – you write `if (ptr != null)` or `if (err != error.Success)` explicitly.

`if` can also be an **expression** returning a value:

```zig
const status = if (x > 0) "positive" else "non-positive";
```

Both branches must produce the same type (or coerce to a common type). This replaces C’s ternary operator `? :` with a more readable form.

#### `if` with Error Unions

Error unions (`!T`) are so common that Zig provides special syntax to capture the payload or the error:

```zig
fn mightFail() !u32 {
    return 123;
}

pub fn example() void {
    // Capture the success value
    if (mightFail()) |value| {
        std.debug.print("got {d}\n", .{value});
    } else |err| {
        std.debug.print("failed with {}\n", .{err});
    }
}
```

This replaces the need for a separate `errdefer` or `catch` when you need to handle both paths explicitly.

#### `while` Loops

Basic `while`:

```zig
var i: u32 = 0;
while (i < 10) {
    std.debug.print("{d} ", .{i});
    i += 1;
}
```

**Continue expression:** A powerful Zig addition – the expression after the loop body runs at the end of each iteration, even if `continue` is called inside:

```zig
var i: u32 = 0;
while (i < 10) : (i += 1) {
    if (i % 2 == 0) continue;
    std.debug.print("{d} ", .{i}); // prints odd numbers
}
```

This keeps the increment logic attached to the loop header, similar to a `for` loop’s third clause in C, but without the confusion of empty bodies.

**`while` with Optionals / Error Unions:** You can loop while an optional has a value:

```zig
var it = std.mem.splitScalar(u8, "a,b,c", ',');
while (it.next()) |part| {
    std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{part});
}
```

Or while an error‑returning function succeeds:

```zig
var reader = std.io.getStdIn().reader();
var buf: [256]u8 = undefined;
while (reader.readUntilDelimiterOrEof(&buf, '\n') catch null) |line| {
    std.debug.print("read: {s}\n", .{line});
}
```

**`else` clause on loops:** Executed when the loop condition becomes false (i.e., normal termination, not via `break`).

```zig
var found = false;
var i: u32 = 0;
while (i < 10) : (i += 1) {
    if (array[i] == target) {
        found = true;
        break;
    }
} else {
    // Executed only if loop finished without `break`
    std.debug.print("not found\n", .{});
}
```

#### `for` Loops

Zig’s `for` iterates over arrays, slices, or ranges:

```zig
const arr = [_]i32{ 10, 20, 30 };
for (arr) |value| {
    std.debug.print("{d} ", .{value});
}

// With index
for (arr, 0..) |value, i| {
    std.debug.print("arr[{d}] = {d}\n", .{ i, value });
}

// Iterate over two sequences in parallel
const names = [_][]const u8{ "Alice", "Bob" };
const ages = [_]u32{ 30, 25 };
for (names, ages) |name, age| {
    std.debug.print("{s} is {d}\n", .{ name, age });
}
```

**Range syntax:** `0..10` produces values 0 through 9. The exclusive upper bound matches slice indexing.

`for` also supports a **continue expression** and an **`else` clause** identical to `while`.

#### `switch`

`switch` in Zig is exhaustive – it must handle every possible value of the input. No fall‑through; each case is separate.

```zig
const color: enum { red, green, blue } = .green;
switch (color) {
    .red => std.debug.print("R\n", .{}),
    .green => std.debug.print("G\n", .{}),
    .blue => std.debug.print("B\n", .{}),
}
```

Multiple cases can share the same body:

```zig
switch (x) {
    1, 2, 3 => std.debug.print("small\n", .{}),
    4, 5, 6 => std.debug.print("medium\n", .{}),
    else => std.debug.print("other\n", .{}),
}
```

The `else` clause covers all remaining values (useful for non‑exhaustive enums or integer ranges).

**Capture payloads** from tagged unions:

```zig
const Value = union(enum) { int: i32, float: f64, none };
var v: Value = .{ .int = 42 };
switch (v) {
    .int => |i| std.debug.print("int {d}\n", .{i}),
    .float => |f| std.debug.print("float {d}\n", .{f}),
    .none => std.debug.print("nothing\n", .{}),
}
```

#### Labeled Blocks and `break`

Any block can be given a label, and `break` can exit that block, optionally returning a value:

```zig
const result = blk: {
    if (some_condition) break :blk 100;
    const y = computeSomething();
    break :blk y * 2;
};
```

This is Zig’s replacement for the “ghetto” `goto` or the `do { … } while(0)` trick in C macros. It also allows early exit from deep nesting without introducing a separate function.

Labeled `break` works inside loops too:

```zig
outer: for (matrix) |row| {
    for (row) |cell| {
        if (cell == 0) break :outer;
    }
}
```

`continue` also supports labels.

