### 2. Under the Hood

#### Lowering to LLVM IR

Zig uses LLVM as its primary backend (the self‑hosted backend is maturing, but the mental model remains similar). Each control flow construct maps predictably to LLVM’s `br` (branch) and `phi` instructions.

**`if` statement:** Generates a `br` on the condition. Example Zig:

```zig
if (x > 0) { a(); } else { b(); }
```

Becomes (simplified LLVM IR):

```llvm
%cmp = icmp sgt i32 %x, 0
br i1 %cmp, label %then, label %else

then:
  call @a()
  br %merge
else:
  call @b()
  br %merge
merge:
  ...
```

**`if` as an expression:** Requires a `phi` node to merge the two possible result values.

**`switch` with dense integer cases:** Compiled into a jump table (array of branch targets) for O(1) dispatch. Sparse cases become a binary search tree or linear cascade, depending on the optimizer’s heuristics. You can inspect this with `zig build-obj` and `llvm-objdump`.

**`while` and `for` loops:** Lowered to a loop block with a conditional branch at the top (or bottom if the compiler unrolls). The continue expression is simply appended at the end of the loop body before the back‑edge branch.

**Comptime elimination:** When the condition of an `if` or `switch` is known at compile time, Zig’s semantic analysis prunes the dead branch completely – no runtime code is generated. This is not an optimization pass; it happens in the front‑end before IR generation.

```zig
const debug = true;
if (debug) {
    // This block disappears entirely when debug == false
    std.debug.print("debug on\n", .{});
}
```

Similarly, `switch` on a `comptime_int` eliminates all but the taken case.

#### Error Union `if` Capture

The `if (foo()) |value| else |err|` syntax desugars to:

```zig
const result = foo();
if (result) |value| {
    // use value
} else |err| {
    // handle err
}
```

Under the hood, the compiler creates an anonymous error union and inspects its tag (the error set or payload presence). This is a runtime check, but the layout of an error union is a simple struct: a payload field and an error code field (or a single pointer when the payload is also a pointer – see Chapter 17). The branch is just a comparison of the error code against zero (zero means success in Zig’s ABI).

#### `defer` and `errdefer` (brief mention)

Although Chapter 16 covers these in depth, note that `defer` pushes a cleanup action onto a hidden stack attached to the current block. When the block exits (normally or via `break`, `return`, `continue`), the deferred statements execute in reverse order. This is not a destructor call – it’s an explicit list of function calls inserted by the compiler at every exit point. The generated code duplicates `defer` cleanups on every return path.

