### 5. Common Mistakes

#### 1. Forgetting that `if` condition must be `bool`

```zig
// Wrong
var ptr: ?*u32 = null;
if (ptr) { … }  // error: expected bool, found ?*u32

// Correct
if (ptr != null) { … }
// Or
if (ptr) |p| { … }  // capture syntax works
```

#### 2. Using `if (err)` instead of error capture

```zig
fn mayFail() !void { return error.Foo; }
const result = mayFail();
if (result) { … }  // error: expected bool, found error union
if (result) |_| { … }  // OK, but captures void payload
if (result) |_| else |err| { … }  // handle both
```

#### 3. Non‑exhaustive `switch` on integers

```zig
const x: u8 = 42;
switch (x) {
    0 => {},
    1 => {},
    // error: switch must handle all values (0…255)
}
// Fix: add `else => {}` or cover entire range
```

#### 4. Using `break` without label in nested loops (breaks only the innermost)

```zig
while (cond1) {
    while (cond2) {
        if (something) break;  // breaks inner loop only
    }
}
// To break outer, label it:
outer: while (cond1) {
    while (cond2) {
        if (something) break :outer;
    }
}
```

#### 5. `continue` skipping continue expression

In a `while (cond) : (continue_expr)`, `continue` still runs the continue expression. This is by design, but newcomers sometimes expect `continue` to jump straight to the condition test.

#### 6. Using `else` on loops when you meant `else` after `if`

```zig
while (cond) {
    if (foo) break;
} else {
    // This runs only if loop didn't break
}
```

It’s easy to confuse with `if-else`. Use comments or refactor to clarify.

#### 7. Capturing the same name multiple times

```zig
if (maybeValue()) |x| {
    // x is in scope here
    _ = x;
} else |err| {
    // err is in scope here
}
// x and err are *not* in scope outside
```

Attempting to use `x` after the `if` block causes a compile error.

