### 7. Best Practices

#### Prefer `if` capture over `.?` or `catch unreachable`

```zig
// Idiomatic
if (maybe_value) |v| {
    use(v);
} else {
    handle_missing();
}

// Avoid unless you know it's never null
const v = maybe_value.?;  // may panic
```

#### Use `switch` with `else` for integers, never `unreachable`

```zig
switch (x) {
    0 => handleZero(),
    1 => handleOne(),
    else => handleOther(x), // always include else for safety
}
```

`unreachable` in a `switch` case is a promise to the compiler that the value never occurs; if it does, the program exhibits undefined behavior.

#### Label blocks to avoid deep nesting

Instead of:

```zig
if (cond1) {
    if (cond2) {
        if (cond3) {
            return compute();
        }
    }
}
return error.Missing;
```

Write:

```zig
const result = blk: {
    if (!cond1) break :blk error.Missing;
    if (!cond2) break :blk error.Missing;
    if (!cond3) break :blk error.Missing;
    break :blk compute();
};
return result;
```

This flattens the logic and makes error paths explicit.

#### Use `while` with continue expression for iterators

```zig
var i: usize = 0;
while (i < n) : (i += 1) {
    // Use i
}
```

This matches the behaviour of C’s `for` but avoids the separate variable scope confusion.

#### For parallel iteration, `for` over multiple slices is clearer than `while`

```zig
for (slice1, slice2) |a, b| {
    // a and b have the same length checked at runtime
}
```

The compiler inserts a runtime check that both slices have equal length – a good safety net.

#### Use `inline for` for compile‑time known sequences

```zig
const fields = std.meta.fields(MyStruct);
inline for (fields) |field| {
    // Generate code for each field
}
```

But avoid `inline` loops over large comptime sequences – compile time can explode.

